question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6494dcea-ff4c-57dc-b8ab-235d70e87dde" :question/math-academy-id "q-119361" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta })+{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =$" :question/worked-solution "First, we expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta })+{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }+{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }}+{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1+{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1+({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =1+1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that, in the last step, we simplified the right-hand side using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta })+{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =2.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329713 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f4dfaf9-6adc-58e2-af62-11084821e994" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327358} {:answer/id #uuid "88ee277f-7d39-51d6-ae8e-804455cc5483" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328112} {:answer/id #uuid "9908e0fd-b391-508e-8dd9-9f1b7572eaab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328256} {:answer/id #uuid "b457a22e-59a5-554f-b4d6-d662134d1479" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328503} {:answer/id #uuid "d0528873-0750-5fae-828d-e2dd5c23f87e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328766}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f4dfaf9-6adc-58e2-af62-11084821e994" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327358} :answer-field/id #uuid "a085cb5f-e013-5736-9ee5-2fef11903a5c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329453}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "770be8a8-209f-5a4a-8f5b-267370ed95fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-43288" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta })=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }+\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }}+\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }} \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that, in the last step, we simplified the right-hand side using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta })=1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329716 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0efce697-3641-5b14-b027-ecd2b195e7bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327067} {:answer/id #uuid "24edcb9d-3e64-5b73-8fd1-94c24bf76145" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327254} {:answer/id #uuid "567f1bcb-a850-52d9-9c67-5c37ad657066" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327683} {:answer/id #uuid "5f9f0214-9783-5eb9-916e-a5dd1fa9da94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327774} {:answer/id #uuid "a00a08ef-de95-5fd8-a811-22199591e5e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328316}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a00a08ef-de95-5fd8-a811-22199591e5e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328316} :answer-field/id #uuid "26560cc9-2457-555a-b1fc-f39375ebfef3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329253}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "efe9bc87-3892-5729-86c2-f5e953c42646" :question/math-academy-id "q-43293" :question/problem "$\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }({sin}^{3}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }({sin}^{3}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot {sin}^{3}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }+\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }}+\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }} \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that, in the last step, we simplified the right-hand side using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }({sin}^{3}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )=1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335809 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d18f305-7184-570c-9038-db639fb9c690" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333104} {:answer/id #uuid "1ef194b7-a3bd-5a62-88d6-f95203c27b49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333262} {:answer/id #uuid "35f8ee1e-2532-55f4-ad12-ee7fd5baa04f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333487} {:answer/id #uuid "76b09143-8785-5fd3-a8de-148adecf998d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334059} {:answer/id #uuid "b2496fe5-9af3-5558-80cb-2b41bd7291a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334554}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d18f305-7184-570c-9038-db639fb9c690" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333104} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8dd284f-c749-5630-9eb7-44cca829df55" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335646}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "33f7f09b-8fbf-5a12-9433-87f274f8c827" :question/math-academy-id "q-65222" :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-{sin}^{2}⁡t.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-{sin}^{2}⁡t & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-{cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that, in the last step, we simplified the right-hand side using ${cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t=1.$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡t(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-{sin}^{2}⁡t=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335810 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29867d59-2029-5173-b108-462543ae8b12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333375} {:answer/id #uuid "2f0ea8ff-9e84-5428-97fe-3086a3798ddd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333427} {:answer/id #uuid "4661ca1a-94ef-5f31-a526-91738f843cdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333641} {:answer/id #uuid "5e3f92c5-ad36-5075-a9ce-39be43e43e00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333851} {:answer/id #uuid "ad1ee91a-2fdf-5e88-a4b9-c50b26f48481" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334514}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e3f92c5-ad36-5075-a9ce-39be43e43e00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333851} :answer-field/id #uuid "26e35aa7-20df-59e9-bd4c-4cb8baf51d6a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335312}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9e0bfa0a-1f0f-581c-9a6b-34627aa91d64" :question/math-academy-id "q-65225" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{sin}⁡t(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1)+{cos}^{2}⁡t=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1)+{cos}^{2}⁡t & ={sin}^{2}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that, in the last step, we simplified the right-hand side using ${cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t=1.$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡t(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1)+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying an Expression by Expanding Parentheses and Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059668 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059434 :question/id #uuid "2138def0-a28a-5018-b93f-c63022825ecb" :question/math-academy-id "e-13411" :question/problem "The point $(a,b)$ lies on the curve $y=x^4.$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y = (x-2)^4-6?$ \n\n- $(a-2, b-6)$\n\n- $(a+2,b+ 6)$\n\n- $(a+2, b-6)$\n\n- $(a-2,b+ 6)$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y = (x-2)^4-6,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, we take the graph $y=x^4.$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally by $2$ units to the right and get the graph of $y=(x-2)^4.$ After this transformation, the point $(a,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(a+2,b).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the result vertically by $6$ units down and obtain the graph of $y = (x-2)^4-6.$ After this transformation, the point $(a+2,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(a+2,b-6).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe are told that the point $(a, b)$ lies on the graph of $y = x^4$, which means:\n\n$$\nb=a^4\n$$\n\nNow, we want to find which point must lie on the graph of:\n\n$$\ny = (x-2)^4-6\n$$\n\nLet’s test what happens when we substitute $x = a + 2$ into this new equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(a+2-2{)}^{4}-6 \\\\  & ={a}^{4}-6 \\\\  & =b-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, when $x = a + 2$, we get $y = b - 6.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0bd940e7-fccc-5811-b07a-11e4843b1785" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048753 :topic/math-academy-id 2841 :topic/title "Combining Reflections With Other Graph Transformations"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060003 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cf13dd1-d493-55fb-94ad-c50574dc8bb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(a,b+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253778} {:answer/id #uuid "71186c58-32dc-5b95-a447-4b4569b59c09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(a,b+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254672} {:answer/id #uuid "a275b10b-0be9-59e6-8c33-ce2b9dcd6958" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(a+1,b+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255104} {:answer/id #uuid "b66ae15a-a2f1-5d79-b5f4-12bbbae7e98e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(a-1,b+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255286} {:answer/id #uuid "e6b76ad7-f3fe-559a-8617-6ec58d9ce1a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(a+2,b+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255701}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a275b10b-0be9-59e6-8c33-ce2b9dcd6958" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(a+1,b+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255104} :answer-field/id #uuid "11178a13-a5c1-5fd6-b0f8-608b3946af04" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255943}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "933f2dda-2343-544a-8ef3-2160dd14e9e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-121220" :question/problem "The point $(a,b)$ lies on the curve $y={x}^{2}.$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=(x-1{)}^{2}+2?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y=(x-1{)}^{2}+1,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, we take the graph $y={x}^{2}.$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally by $1$ unit to the right and get the graph of $y=(x-1{)}^{2}.$ After this transformation, the point $(a,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(a+1,b).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the result vertically by $2$ units up and obtain the graph of $y=(x-1{)}^{2}+2.$ After this transformation, the point $(a+1,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(a+1,b+2).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(a,b)$ lies on the graph of $y={x}^{2}$, we have that ${a}^{2}=b.$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=(x-1{)}^{2}+2.$ Substituting $x=a+1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(a+1-1{)}^{2}+2 \\\\  & ={a}^{2}+2 \\\\  & =b+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(a+1,b+2)$ must lie on $y=(x-1{)}^{2}+2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060258 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "215b733e-02f5-559d-b497-8039bd0abddb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290773} {:answer/id #uuid "40347c00-b783-5421-939f-ab3ef7825eb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290900} {:answer/id #uuid "5451ee39-3132-55be-b496-cccbb1ecfcd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290991} {:answer/id #uuid "669f54dd-330e-5187-a457-8ec777fa2db4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291068} {:answer/id #uuid "832359a3-815f-50ef-8abf-011a2185b994" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291186}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "669f54dd-330e-5187-a457-8ec777fa2db4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291068} :answer-field/id #uuid "7865af2d-939b-59a2-9e75-cdf7738a3599" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292113}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e4d7f8b8-ba0a-5db0-954f-1969ddfefa81" :question/math-academy-id "q-26398" :question/problem "The point $(1,4)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x).$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=f(x-1)+1?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y=f(x-1)+1,$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x).$\n\n- Then, shift the graph horizontally by $1$ unit to the right and get the graph of \n$y=f(x-1).$  After this transformation, the point $(1,4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(1+1,4)=(2,4).\n$$\n\n- Finally,  we shift the result vertically by $1$  unit up and obtain the graph of\n$y=f(x-1)+1.$ After this transformation, the point $(2,4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(2,4+1)=(2,5).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(1,4)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x)$, we have that $f(1)=4.$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=f(x-1)+1.$ Substituting $x=2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =f(2-1)+1 \\\\  & =f(1)+1 \\\\  & =4+1 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(2,5)$ must lie on $y=f(x-1)+1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233288 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "00e41b54-1b06-55e4-ba21-6bbf8797df49" :question/math-academy-id "q-121381" :question/problem "The point $(a,b)$ lies on the curve $y=\\sqrt{x}.$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=\\sqrt{x-1}-1?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y=\\sqrt{x-1}-1,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, we take the graph $y=\\sqrt{x}.$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally by $1$ unit to the right and get the graph of $y=\\sqrt{x-1}.$ After this transformation, the point $(a,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(a+1,b).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the result vertically by $1$ unit down and obtain the graph of $y=\\sqrt{x-1}-1.$ After this transformation, the point $(a+1,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(a+1,b-1).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(a,b)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\sqrt{x}$, we have that $\\sqrt{a}=b.$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=\\sqrt{x-1}-1.$ Substituting $x=a+1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{a+1-1}-1 \\\\  & =\\sqrt{a}-1 \\\\  & =b-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(a+1,b-1)$ must lie on $y=\\sqrt{x-1}-1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233289 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7d1f17ba-35cc-58b5-9aa2-08d577267c2c" :question/math-academy-id "q-121393" :question/problem "The point $(a,b)$ lies on the curve $y={x}^{3}.$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=2(x+2)^{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y=2(x+2)^{3},$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, we take the graph $y={x}^{3}.$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally by $2$ units to the left and get the graph of $y=(x+2)^{3}.$ After this transformation, the point $(a,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(a-2,b).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we stretch the graph by a scale factor of  $2$ parallel to the $y$ -axis and get the graph of  $y=2(x+2)^{3}.$ After this transformation, the point $(a-2,b)$ will be mapped to the point \n\n$$\n(a-2,2b).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(a,b)$ lies on the graph of $y={x}^{3}$, we have that ${a}^{3}=b.$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=2(x+2)^{3}.$ Substituting $x=a-2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2(a-2+2)^{3} \\\\  & =2{a}^{3} \\\\  & =2b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(a-2,2b)$ must lie on $y=2(x+2)^{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233534 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4a80933c-10f7-51d8-9b1a-039e3550e2d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-158540" :question/problem "The point $(-1,1)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x).$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=3f(x+2)?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y=3f(x+2),$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x).$\n\n- Then, shift the graph horizontally by $2$ unit to the left and get the graph of \n$y=f(x+2).$  After this transformation, the point $(-1,1)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-1-2,1)=(-3,1).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we stretch the result by a scale factor of $3$ parallel to the y-axis and get the graph of \n$y=3f(x+2).$ After this transformation, the point $(-3,1)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-3,3\\cdot 1)=(-3,3).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(-1,1)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x)$, we have that $f(-1)=1.$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=3f(x+2).$ Substituting $x=-3$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3f(-3+2) \\\\  & =3f(-1) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-3,3)$ must lie on $y=3f(x+2).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256539 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "165e3ec5-5bc9-5cba-9c8a-6b6ebe75a58b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(a-2,b+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253863} {:answer/id #uuid "5828d10b-52d2-519e-94d5-63d2d5b4c550" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(a-2,b-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254445} {:answer/id #uuid "74fc90a5-c321-5fe2-a988-759338bbb191" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(a-2,3b)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254707} {:answer/id #uuid "b59d2443-c7bf-54dc-9108-20e2c63d903b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(a+2,b+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255271} {:answer/id #uuid "c709185f-e827-59eb-bd52-699c51a7e1b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(a+2,b-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255448}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b59d2443-c7bf-54dc-9108-20e2c63d903b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(a+2,b+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255271} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca534256-c181-5ac0-b56d-2d5a591a2172" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256283}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9b584cb7-ff23-5cf8-a53c-0371ad6cd2e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-121383" :question/problem "The point $(a,b)$ lies on the curve $y={2}^{x}.$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y={2}^{x-2}+3?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y={2}^{x-2}+3,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, we take the graph $y={2}^{x}.$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally by $2$ units to the right and get the graph of $y={2}^{x-2}.$ After this transformation, the point $(a,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(a+2,b).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the result vertically by $3$ units up and obtain the graph of $y={2}^{x-2}+3.$ After this transformation, the point $(a+2,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(a+2,b+3).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(a,b)$ lies on the graph of $y={2}^{x}$, we have that ${2}^{a}=b.$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y={2}^{x-2}+3.$ Substituting $x=a+2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={2}^{a+2-2}+3 \\\\  & ={2}^{a}+3 \\\\  & =b+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(a+2,b+3)$ must lie on $y={2}^{x-2}+3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256540 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44c21c55-17bb-5c41-88d1-6c299c881b29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(a-2,b-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254253} {:answer/id #uuid "80df87fd-c227-5716-9dc2-6b062371184a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(a-3,b-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254808} {:answer/id #uuid "b5f3a22f-dbdf-598f-a34c-1777eda4f583" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(a+3,b-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255276} {:answer/id #uuid "bbff3184-f777-51ba-8744-de29245ae71f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(a+3,b+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255349} {:answer/id #uuid "f87700c2-b05d-5438-9cf7-184870d44569" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(a-3,b+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255846}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80df87fd-c227-5716-9dc2-6b062371184a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(a-3,b-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254808} :answer-field/id #uuid "074aa316-c74e-53ab-a8c8-8f76eb00e9c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255928}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "19d2f443-e38d-5ded-a277-4288cfd36f31" :question/math-academy-id "q-121385" :question/problem "The point $(a,b)$ lies on the curve $y=|x|.$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=|x+3|-2?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y=|x+3|-2,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, we take the graph $y=|x|.$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally by $3$ units to the left and get the graph of $y=|x+3|.$ After this transformation, the point $(a,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(a-3,b).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the result vertically by $2$ units down and obtain the graph of $y=|x+3|-2.$ After this transformation, the point $(a-3,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(a-3,b-2).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(a,b)$ lies on the graph of $y=|x|$, we have that $|a|=b.$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=|x+3|-2.$ Substituting $x=a-3$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =|a-3+3|-2 \\\\  & =|a|-2 \\\\  & =b-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(a-3,b-2)$ must lie on $y=|x+3|-2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256541 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e5bc30c-2abb-52a7-bb51-cb184ad226c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{a}{2},b-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254343} {:answer/id #uuid "6cad43fd-a241-5e56-82fc-2eeac4a6bb5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(a+2,b+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254635} {:answer/id #uuid "81df30f7-1414-57bb-a6ef-282731df4980" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({a}^{3/2},b+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254821} {:answer/id #uuid "adde4716-7e5d-5b0d-b0ca-454d703c35b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2a,b+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255204} {:answer/id #uuid "f6019e36-937d-5523-941c-72dc4b368337" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{a}{2},b+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255822}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "adde4716-7e5d-5b0d-b0ca-454d703c35b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2a,b+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255204} :answer-field/id #uuid "4647e489-d352-5af5-8c97-6318e3c26f3b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256054}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d40ea2d8-4f8c-52c6-b88c-21d92eb5a3d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-121399" :question/problem "The point $(a,b)$ lies on the curve $y={3}^{x}.$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y={3}^{x/2}+1?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y={3}^{x/2}+1,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, we take the graph $y={3}^{x}.$\n\n- Then, we stretch the graph by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $x$-axis and get the graph of  $y={3}^{x/2}.$ After this transformation, the point $(a,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(2a,b).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the result vertically by $1$ unit up and obtain the graph of $y={3}^{x/2}+1.$ After this transformation, the point $(2a,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(2a,b+1).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(a,b)$ lies on the graph of $y={3}^{x}$, we have that ${3}^{a}=b.$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y={3}^{x/2}+1.$ Substituting $x=2a$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={3}^{(2a)/2}+1 \\\\  & ={3}^{a}+1 \\\\  & =b+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(2a,b+1)$ must lie on $y={3}^{x/2}+1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292672 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33c00c15-b13b-5bcc-8be2-21e93f4c417e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290839} {:answer/id #uuid "487a2755-1c9b-50dc-a9ef-825d319c08d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290930} {:answer/id #uuid "5a5b7f1c-6ca6-519d-9324-afd97504caa0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291021} {:answer/id #uuid "bfbeb462-5ad4-5751-9859-039c2c20e85a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291457} {:answer/id #uuid "f4debbbb-a978-5aa2-807b-d09d5e6771d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291662}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33c00c15-b13b-5bcc-8be2-21e93f4c417e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290839} :answer-field/id #uuid "337977c2-17f5-5416-91d9-a9bbf15f1e4e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291907}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6eb852b1-3670-5b7e-89d9-1b29c405f01a" :question/math-academy-id "q-26594" :question/problem "The point  $(3,1)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x).$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=-f(x)-1?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y=-f(x)-1,$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x).$\n\n- Then, we reflect the graph across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(3,1)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(3,-(1))=(3,-1).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the result vertically by $1$ unit down and obtain the graph of $y=-f(x)-1.$ After this transformation, the point $(3,-1)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(3,-1-1)=(3,-2).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(3,1)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x)$, we have that $f(3)=1.$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=-f(x)-1.$ Substituting $x=3$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-f(3)-1 \\\\  & =-(1)-1 \\\\  & =-1-1 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(3,-2)$ must lie on $y=-f(x)-1.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Point That Lies on a Transformed Curve"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059669 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059435 :question/id #uuid "55fd531a-92e2-5f75-8c40-585d4f71bf4c" :question/math-academy-id "e-13412" :question/problem "The point $(3, 0)$ lies on the curve $y = f(x) - 1.$ What point **must** lie on $y = -f(x) + 3?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y = -f(x) + 3,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y = f(x) - 1.$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph by $1$ unit up and get the graph of $y=f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(3,0)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(3,0+1)=(3,1).\n$$\n\n- Next, we reflect the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(3,1)$ will be mapped  to the point\n\n$$\n(3,-(1)) = (3,-1).\n$$\n\n- Finally, shift the result by $3$ units up and obtain the graph of $y = -f(x) + 3.$ After this transformation, the point $(3,-1)$ will be mapped to the point \n\n$$\n(3,-1+3)=(3,2).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(3, 0)$ lies on the graph of $y = f(x)-1$, we have that \n\n$$\nf(3)-1 = 0\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nf(3)=1.\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y = -f(x) + 3.$ Substituting $x = 3$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-f(3)+3 \\\\  & =-1+3 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(3, 2)$ must lie on $y = -f(x) + 3.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "38b450bd-b43a-507c-9bbe-0aaf7a64a3ed" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060006 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "123d4004-5c38-5292-afe0-3926ee0d0bb4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2, 0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056395} {:answer/id #uuid "215d0348-3325-5b0f-b430-be17a1c52b8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2, 3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056396} {:answer/id #uuid "37548807-d256-5fc9-98ef-bbc3e9fc2a85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2, 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056397} {:answer/id #uuid "5376b742-5988-5f01-98d6-a68f5ec75b57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056398} {:answer/id #uuid "5da47774-b4c3-5656-a5f3-c6639c1c26fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056399}] :answer-field/id #uuid "437656e2-e6a9-5e24-ad04-71e3bec79dec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056394}] :question/id #uuid "35f6ccdb-baf2-5629-ace1-fbbc52cdd1b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-121405" :question/problem "The point $(-2, 4)$ lies on the curve $y = 2f(x)$. Which of the following points **must** lie on $y =-f(x) + 2$?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060259 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8bad3011-eec4-5bd6-9f12-9de90977d847" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1, 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058177} {:answer/id #uuid "dba39df7-83e7-5fab-846e-119c803d8ee2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1, 0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058178} {:answer/id #uuid "e1b7ee60-2fd8-523e-8b42-4824fc438dfc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058179} {:answer/id #uuid "272b5946-719d-5b75-850b-893b6e80fde6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1, 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058180} {:answer/id #uuid "6edc1855-3018-5684-a7e0-1d2d3b236b62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058181}] :answer-field/id #uuid "0e9c2bb2-bb19-5c69-a07c-605a418546e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058176}] :question/id #uuid "49ac0e7b-6d64-5ad3-b75e-0edbe3724d3a" :question/math-academy-id "q-26595" :question/problem "The point $(-1, 1)$ lies on the curve $y = f(x) + 1$. Which of the following points **must** lie on $y =-f(x) - 1$?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233290 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "66ee65e4-7115-5fe1-b74b-2344562d97c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-121431" :question/problem "The point  $(0,1)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x+2).$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=-f(x)+2?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nWe first find a point corresponding to $(0,1)$ on $y=f(x).$ Then, we find a corresponding point on $y=-f(x)+2:$\n\n- To plot the graph of $y=f(x),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x+2).$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally $2$ unit to the right  and get the graph of $y=f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(0,1)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(0+2,1)=(2,1).\n$$\n\n- Next, to plot the graph of $y=-f(x)+2,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x).$\n\n- Then, we reflect the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(2,1)$ will be mapped  to the point\n\n$$\n(2,-(1))=(2,-1).\n$$\n\n- Next, we shift the result vertically by $2$ units up and obtain the graph of $y=-f(x)+2.$ After this transformation, the point $(2,-1)$ will be mapped to the point \n\n$$\n(2,-1+2)=(2,1).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(0,1)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x+2)$, we have that \n\n$$\nf(0+2)=1\\,⟹\\,f(2)=1.\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=-f(x)+2.$ Substituting $x=2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-f(2)+2 \\\\  & =-(1)+2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(2,1)$ must lie on $y=-f(x)+2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233535 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1b6d8898-251c-5832-a715-c1e0b2d67196" :question/math-academy-id "q-158542" :question/problem "The point  $(0,3)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x-1).$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=f(x)+2?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nWe first find a point corresponding to $(0,3)$ on $y=f(x).$ Then, we find a corresponding point on $y=f(x)+2:$\n\n- To plot the graph of $y=f(x),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x-1).$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally $1$ unit to the left and get the graph of $y=f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(0,3)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(0-1,3)=(-1,3).\n$$\n\n- Next, to plot the graph of $y=f(x)+2,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x).$\n\n- Next, we shift the result vertically by $2$ units up and obtain the graph of $y=f(x)+2.$ After this transformation, the point $(-1,3)$ will be mapped to the point \n\n$$\n(-1,3+2)=(-1,5).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(0,3)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x-1)$, we have that \n\n$$\nf(0-1)=3\\,⟹\\,f(-1)=3.\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=f(x)+2.$ Substituting $x=-1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =f(-1)+2 \\\\  & =3+2 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-1,5)$ must lie on $y=f(x)+2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233536 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bcbd4637-6a8f-52c9-a823-7c7a1c3283b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-158546" :question/problem "The point  $(2,1)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x-2).$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=3-f(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nWe first find a point corresponding to $(2,1)$ on $y=f(x).$ Then, we find a corresponding point on $y=3-f(x):$\n\n- To plot the graph of $y=f(x),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x-2).$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally $2$ units to the left and get the graph of $y=f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(2,1)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(2-2,1)=(0,1).\n$$\n\n- Next, to plot the graph of $y=3-f(x),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x).$\n\n- Then, we reflect the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(0,1)$ will be mapped  to the point\n\n$$\n(0,-(1))=(0,-1).\n$$\n\n- Next, we shift the result vertically by $3$ units up and obtain the graph of $y=3-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(0,-1)$ will be mapped to the point \n\n$$\n(0,-1+3)=(0,2).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(2,1)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x-2)$, we have that \n\n$$\nf(2-2)=1\\,⟹\\,f(0)=1.\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=3-f(x).$ Substituting $x=0$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3-f(0) \\\\  & =3-1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(0,2)$ must lie on $y=3-f(x).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233700 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d89896ee-0650-5ce4-90d0-9b6eb4318030" :question/math-academy-id "q-26597" :question/problem "The point  $(0,4)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x)+2.$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=-f(x)-3?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y=-f(x)-3,$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x)+2.$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph vertically by $2$ unit down and get the graph of $y=f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(0,4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(0,4-2)=(0,2).\n$$\n\n- Next, we reflect the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(0,2)$ will be mapped  to the point\n\n$$\n(0,-(2))=(0,-2).\n$$\n\n- Finally, shift the result vertically by $3$ units down and obtain the graph of $y=-f(x)-3.$ After this transformation, the point $(0,-2)$ will be mapped to the point \n\n$$\n(0,-2-3)=(0,-5).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(0,4)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x)+2$, we have that \n\n$$\nf(0)+2=4\\,⟹\\,f(0)=2.\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=-f(x)-3.$ Substituting $x=0$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-f(0)-3 \\\\  & =-2-3 \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(0,-5)$ must lie on $y=-f(x)-3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253566 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42a70a80-9905-5343-a34c-136a375e25ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251108} {:answer/id #uuid "9d7c8745-888b-5fc6-9d1a-fc1686129698" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251913} {:answer/id #uuid "a8ce837a-8964-565e-9e2d-f825bac56dba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252014} {:answer/id #uuid "d6b4c14b-cbd8-5494-b7e3-3692ec97eb35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252455} {:answer/id #uuid "e0fa8370-7c51-5755-8595-d094b48e3a78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252534}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d7c8745-888b-5fc6-9d1a-fc1686129698" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251913} :answer-field/id #uuid "2019c876-575f-5be0-a410-079acd66193f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252863}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4d38949b-9d50-5e0f-bb63-ea8ff36967c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-119308" :question/problem "The point $(2,3)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x)-1.$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=-f(x)+4?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y=-f(x)+4,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x)-1.$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph vertically by $1$ unit up and get the graph of $y=f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(2,3)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(2,3+1)=(2,4).\n$$\n\n- Next, we reflect the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(2,4)$ will be mapped  to the point\n\n$$\n(2,-(4))=(2,-4).\n$$\n\n- Finally, shift the result vertically by $4$ units up and obtain the graph of $y=-f(x)+4.$ After this transformation, the point $(2,-4)$ will be mapped to the point \n\n$$\n(2,-4+4)=(2,0).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(2,3)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x)-1$, we have that \n\n$$\nf(2)-1=3\\,⟹\\,f(2)=4.\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=-f(x)+4.$ Substituting $x=2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-f(2)+4 \\\\  & =-4+4 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(2,0)$ must lie on $y=-f(x)+4.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256542 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34d52287-c3ec-5c1d-bc8c-f42f5d1b1173" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254122} {:answer/id #uuid "48193e72-9f23-5387-b843-0b954ae77279" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254280} {:answer/id #uuid "775e9c8f-aa49-5c77-bf40-dadfb16663be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254726} {:answer/id #uuid "83e30582-b220-5945-90a6-dc420162278d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254842} {:answer/id #uuid "df43e300-d37a-5ddf-bd7d-9b8787a369ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255652}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "48193e72-9f23-5387-b843-0b954ae77279" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254280} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0cc13c9-29f6-55db-9718-7dfe7fa0c982" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256348}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "558ef869-beed-50a3-ac33-63c76838d181" :question/math-academy-id "q-121407" :question/problem "The point  $(-3,4)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x+1).$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=-f(x)+2?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nWe first find a point corresponding to $(-3,4)$ on $y=f(x).$ Then, we find a corresponding point on $y=-f(x)+2:$\n\n- To plot the graph of $y=f(x),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x+1).$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally $1$ unit to the right  and get the graph of $y=f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(-3,4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-3+1,4)=(-2,4).\n$$\n\n- Next, to plot the graph of $y=-f(x)+2,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x).$\n\n- Then, we reflect the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(-2,4)$ will be mapped  to the point\n\n$$\n(-2,-(4))=(-2,-4).\n$$\n\n- Next, we shift the result vertically by $2$ units up and obtain the graph of $y=-f(x)+2.$ After this transformation, the point $(-2,-4)$ will be mapped to the point \n\n$$\n(-2,-4+2)=(-2,-2).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(-3,4)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x+1)$, we have that \n\n$$\nf(-3+1)=4\\,⟹\\,f(-2)=4.\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=-f(x)+2.$ Substituting $x=-2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-f(-2)+2 \\\\  & =-4+2 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-2,-2)$ must lie on $y=-f(x)+2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256543 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30f927d8-7469-5f46-bcd8-ceb6220bf583" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254097} {:answer/id #uuid "5679b0cb-b917-5834-9cfc-21236c05bf34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254431} {:answer/id #uuid "8ddf4522-8bdc-5052-93ac-81ce5d766651" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254921} {:answer/id #uuid "d96d1372-c430-5db2-a838-5534030331cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255597} {:answer/id #uuid "dc8c348e-dc48-52a8-a7a2-dcc717c081b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255624}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "30f927d8-7469-5f46-bcd8-ceb6220bf583" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254097} :answer-field/id #uuid "65ce4959-7406-5c8f-b9dc-e08ef35e4173" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256112}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e35c2dec-10c0-57fb-accb-533118e28579" :question/math-academy-id "q-121414" :question/problem "The point  $(4,8)$ lies on the curve $y=4f(x).$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=-f(x)+3?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nWe first find a point corresponding to $(4,8)$ on $y=f(x).$ Then, we find a corresponding point on $y=-f(x)+3:$\n\n- To plot the graph of $y=f(x),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=4f(x).$\n\n- Then, we stretch the graph by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{4}$ parallel to the $y$-axis and get the graph of $y=f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(4,8)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(4,\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 8)=(4,2).\n$$\n\n- Next, to plot the graph of $y=-f(x)+3,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x).$\n\n- Then, we reflect the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(4,2)$ will be mapped  to the point\n\n$$\n(4,-(2))=(4,-2).\n$$\n\n- Next, we shift the result vertically by $3$ units up and obtain the graph of $y=-f(x)+3.$ After this transformation, the point $(4,-2)$ will be mapped to the point \n\n$$\n(4,-2+3)=(4,1).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(4,8)$ lies on the graph of $y=4f(x)$, we have that \n\n$$\n4f(4)=8\\,⟹\\,f(4)=2.\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=-f(x)+3.$ Substituting $x=4$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-f(4)+3 \\\\  & =-2+3 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(4,1)$ must lie on $y=-f(x)+3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256544 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b36618b-e713-53a3-ac40-6a8f5599cd4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254899} {:answer/id #uuid "a4b1c168-5fba-5920-9504-8801e7ea3407" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255124} {:answer/id #uuid "b929824d-a081-5202-b41a-f097c3bbd269" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255313} {:answer/id #uuid "b939c68f-9f1f-5b41-b64e-a890960575a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,-8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255316} {:answer/id #uuid "ddeface4-f07f-5f2a-bf40-5f85d82a4184" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255637}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a4b1c168-5fba-5920-9504-8801e7ea3407" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255124} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3896048-4abe-53e6-9858-4e169d6c5730" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256246}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "517a1517-e6eb-55b1-8c36-85bba8616819" :question/math-academy-id "q-121418" :question/problem "The point  $(2,3)$ lies on the curve $y=\\frac{1}{3}f(x).$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=-f(x)+5?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nWe first find a point corresponding to $(2,3)$ on $y=f(x).$ Then, we find a corresponding point on $y=-f(x)+5:$\n\n- To plot the graph of $y=f(x),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=\\frac{1}{3}f(x).$\n\n- Then, we stretch the graph by a scale factor of $3$ parallel to the $y$-axis and get the graph of $y=f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(2,3)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(2,3\\cdot 3)=(2,9).\n$$\n\n- Next, to plot the graph of $y=-f(x)+5,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x).$\n\n- Then, we reflect the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(2,9)$ will be mapped  to the point\n\n$$\n(2,-(9))=(2,-9).\n$$\n\n- Next, we shift the result vertically by $5$ units up and obtain the graph of $y=-f(x)+5.$ After this transformation, the point $(2,-9)$ will be mapped to the point \n\n$$\n(2,-9+5)=(2,-4).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(2,3)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{3}f(x)$, we have that \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{3}f(2)=3\\,⟹\\,f(2)=9.\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=-f(x)+5.$ Substituting $x=2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-f(2)+5 \\\\  & =-(9)+5 \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(2,-4)$ must lie on $y=-f(x)+5.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292650 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "036b3467-551b-5d3e-99f8-f34df741c278" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290655} {:answer/id #uuid "1789665b-9485-59bf-ac4a-24f4ca8e7c6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290731} {:answer/id #uuid "4d22bf21-6fd1-521b-8cf6-ef9303eb6596" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290960} {:answer/id #uuid "8d7a2478-3275-509d-a6c9-6e581e21989f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291237} {:answer/id #uuid "d66724d1-9c6b-5173-a28b-c720d8d3a432" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291530}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "036b3467-551b-5d3e-99f8-f34df741c278" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290655} :answer-field/id #uuid "20dcd5d3-0f90-560e-bed2-53e688d6b92b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291856}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "145a9fb2-d278-526f-9014-c06bdf23b5a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-26408" :question/problem "The point $(-1,3)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x)+1.$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=-f(x)+2?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y=-f(x)+2,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x)+1.$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph vertically by $1$ unit down and get the graph of $y=f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(-1,3)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-1,3-1)=(-1,2).\n$$\n\n- Next, we reflect the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(-1,2)$ will be mapped  to the point\n\n$$\n(-1,-(2))=(-1,-2).\n$$\n\n- Finally, shift the result vertically by $2$ units up and obtain the graph of $y=-f(x)+2.$ After this transformation, the point $(-1,-2)$ will be mapped to the point \n\n$$\n(-1,-2+2)=(-1,0).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(-1,3)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x)+1$, we have that \n\n$$\nf(-1)+1=3\\,⟹\\,f(-1)=2.\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=-f(x)+2.$ Substituting $x=-1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-f(-1)+2 \\\\  & =-2+2 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-1,0)$ must lie on $y=-f(x)+2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292673 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f5bb3ff-2bf2-59a6-a9dc-028e36b29e5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290821} {:answer/id #uuid "6a0dffee-d291-5706-b099-bf26056b0a79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291085} {:answer/id #uuid "745f11b3-4abb-5778-b95c-05bfed92c9ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291130} {:answer/id #uuid "add1ba6b-56e1-507d-81c4-d7cc7dd66d71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291387} {:answer/id #uuid "f811fc7c-86fc-528d-a6a8-cba9ceee139f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291677}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "add1ba6b-56e1-507d-81c4-d7cc7dd66d71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291387} :answer-field/id #uuid "e46bb93d-f096-5e20-be4c-e4f183d28feb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292449}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4ee92c98-a45f-55db-8092-7db7c00e5a4e" :question/math-academy-id "q-26596" :question/problem "The point  $(3,2)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x)-2.$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=-f(x)+2?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nWe first find a point corresponding to $(3,2)$ on $y=f(x).$ Then, we find a corresponding point on $y=-f(x)+2:$\n\n- To plot the graph of $y=f(x),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x)-2.$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph vertically by $2$ units up and get the graph of $y=f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(3,2)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(3,2+2)=(3,4).\n$$\n\n- Next, to plot the graph of $y=-f(x)+2,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x).$\n\n- Then, we reflect the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(3,4)$ will be mapped  to the point\n\n$$\n(3,-(4))=(3,-4).\n$$\n\n- Next, we shift the result vertically by $2$ units up and obtain the graph of $y=-f(x)+2.$ After this transformation, the point $(3,-4)$ will be mapped to the point \n\n$$\n(3,-4+2)=(3,-2).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(3,2)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x)-2$, we have that \n\n$$\nf(3)-2=2\\,⟹\\,f(3)=4.\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=-f(x)+2.$ Substituting $x=3$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-f(3)+2 \\\\  & =-4+2 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(3,-2)$ must lie on $y=-f(x)+2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292679 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e45f316-de9f-5078-a264-4bec8ddfa33d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291105} {:answer/id #uuid "81083a07-3ca9-51a3-aef1-cacbb9dc674e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291178} {:answer/id #uuid "818351c2-9cf1-5db6-910a-2037182a0665" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291179} {:answer/id #uuid "d8f07a2a-5cfb-5180-a1fd-c1ba356692a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,-8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291540} {:answer/id #uuid "f8be8001-d31c-55ee-acbf-2ca45e8e2140" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291681}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "81083a07-3ca9-51a3-aef1-cacbb9dc674e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291178} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4fe658c-44a8-558f-a2d7-7429144d854f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292500}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d646706a-b186-5587-8de9-3f31bf5fbf11" :question/math-academy-id "q-26726" :question/problem "The point  $(4,2)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x)+2.$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=-f(x)+4?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y=-f(x)+4,$ we apply the following steps:  \n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x)+2.$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph vertically by $2$ units down and get the graph of $y=f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(4,2)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(4,2-2)=(4,0).\n$$\n\n- Next, we reflect the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(4,0)$ will be mapped  to the point\n\n$$\n(4,-(0))=(4,0).\n$$\n\n- Finally, shift the result vertically by $4$ units up and obtain the graph of $y=-f(x)+4.$ After this transformation, the point $(4,0)$ will be mapped to the point \n\n$$\n(4,0+4)=(4,4).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(4,2)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x)+2$, we have that \n\n$$\nf(4)+2=2\\,⟹\\,f(4)=0.\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=-f(x)+4.$ Substituting $x=4$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-f(4)+4 \\\\  & =-0+4 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(4,4)$ must lie on $y=-f(x)+4.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Point That Lies on a Transformed Curve Given Another Transformed Curve"}]}
